Where the trans-provincial power grid management departments and provincial power grid management departments need to adjust the power generation and supply plans due to any of the following circumstances, they shall notify the relevant departments of the local people's governments:
(1) Insufficient inflow water volume for large and medium-sized hydropower plants (stations);
(2) Fuel shortage for thermal power plants;
(3) Other circumstances requiring adjustment of the power generation and supply plans.

Where any of the following emergency situations occurs, dispatchers on duty may adjust the daily dispatching plans for power generation and supply, issue instructions such as limiting power supply, adjusting the power output of power plants, starting or stopping generating units; and may issue dispatching instructions to the units on duty for operation of power plants and substations within the same power grid:
(1) A major accident occurs in power generation or supply equipment or an accident occurs in the power grid;
(2) The frequency or voltage of the power grid exceeds the specified range;
(3) The load of power transmission and transformation equipment exceeds the specified value;
(4) The power flow of main transmission lines exceeds the specified stability limit;
(5) Other emergency situations threatening the safe operation of the power grid.

Article 27 Where any of the following acts is committed in violation of these Regulations, the person in charge and the person directly responsible shall be given administrative sanctions by their unit or the superior authority:
(1) failing to execute the power generation and supply dispatch plan issued by the superior dispatching institution without the permission of the superior dispatching institution;
(2) failing to execute the maintenance plan approved by the relevant dispatching institution;
(3) failing to execute dispatch orders or the measures for ensuring the safety of the power grid issued by the dispatching institution;
(4) failing to truthfully report the operation status of the power grid;
(5) failing to truthfully report the execution of dispatch orders;
(6) where the dispatcher on duty of the dispatch system neglects duties or engages in malpractices for personal gain but such act does not yet constitute a crime.
